# family: flat
# bridge: A(law-restatement)
# regime: exact (the same assertions over the same session, value for value)
# instrument-first: the GDScript suite this replaces, case for case
# baseline: tests/unit/core
# baseline-count: 77
#
# BASELINE RAISED 2026-08-10, from 74, by the three cases
# test_decision_seams.gd adds. A baseline-count freezes what the family held
# when it crossed so a later DELETION cannot hide; coverage ADDED afterwards
# has to be absorbed here or the arithmetic reads as a lie in the other
# direction. The three are new laws for
# NetwMultiplayer.overrides_seam, which is what every native prediction
# decision defers to, and they replace nothing.
#
# The unit is not a class. Every other ledger licenses a GDScript suite's
# deletion because its SUBJECT went native; this one licenses a suite's deletion
# because the TEST TIER moved and the subject did not. Interest and display are
# already native, the flat verbs over them are still GDScript, and these five
# laws assert the same things about the same session from C++ instead of from
# GdUnit.
#
# WHAT MADE IT POSSIBLE, after three refusals. EntityDecl was refused at the
# table batch, again at liveness, and again at the W5-B open, each time on the
# same measured ground: public surface with no consumer. The consumer arrived
# with the decision to move the corpus off GDScript, and the gap that was left
# turned out to be ONE VERB. entity_bind_route already admitted a wrapperless
# row (netw_multiplayer.gd), so the blocker recorded in the interface design's
# §3.3 was stale; what actually refused a record-plane entity was every facet
# that needs a live wrapper -- entity_add_property_set answered ERR_UNAVAILABLE,
# scene_declare answered ERR_DOES_NOT_EXIST, and entity_grant_control did
# nothing at all. entity_bind_node is the verb that promotes the row, and the
# promotion itself was already written: liveness_shell.gd's bind_route takes a
# wrapperless record over onto the arriving wrapper, same RID, same route.
#
# WHY THE ROWS READ AS PARITY RATHER THAN AS A PORT. Nothing about the
# implementation moved, so there is no shadow to read zero and no determinism
# floor to re-measure. What the bridge claims is narrower and checkable: each
# native case asserts the same values its GDScript original asserted, and the
# session under both is the same GDScript session object.
#
# WHAT THE MOVE COST THE CASES, three things, all of them the tier's rules
# rather than this suite's:
#
#   the tree died      MultiplayerTree existed to hand the suite an api and to
#                      host four nodes. LoopbackRig hands the api and
#                      EntityDecl declares the entity, so the four-line
#                      prologue every case opened with is one line that names
#                      what the case is about.
#   numbers cannot     a failing CHECK on an int segfaults the hosted runner in
#   be compared        the numeric facet, so every numeric comparison reads
#   directly           NETW_CHECK_EQ and every Godot-typed one is wrapped in
#                      bool(). This is netw_test.h's rule, paid here.
#   no [Hosted] tag    the rig loads the session script from res://, which the
#                      module tier cannot see. A [Hosted] case that cannot
#                      construct its api would read as a pass there.
#
# TWO OBSERVATIONS RECORDED RATHER THAN FIXED. The per-case leak census flags
# all five new cases at +157 to +175 objects, exactly as it already flags the
# five transport rig cases and the two session ones: the library build cannot
# flush the delete queue, which is the same known reason the clock ledger
# refuses to promote that census to a gate. And the hosted tier prints six
# "Interest entity key must be positive" errors during the run, from the
# pre-existing null-slot law, because ERR_PRINT_OFF is a no-op outside the
# module tier. Neither is this batch's and neither is silent.
#
# THE REST OF tests/unit/core CARRIES, and the reasons are not one reason.
# ONE suite awaits a coroutine no native tier can drive (test_async_mutex.gd,
# over a GDScript utility whose lock IS the coroutine); four drive a tree, a
# component or the join path; two assert on the logger's own output; one
# assembles PackedScenes, which is the affirmative permanent carry;
# test_settle_queue.gd drives the shell session's relay onto a queue that is
# already native; and test_flat_prediction_lagcomp.gd carries because the sim
# family crosses with its own port rather than with this one.
#
# RE-STATED at ZU-11 against the measured tree. The settle-queue rows read
# "awaits a promise, which no native tier can drive" and that was false twice
# over: five of the six cases await nothing at all, the sixth awaits gdUnit's
# own assert_error, and the subject is NetwMultiplayerCore's settle_schedule /
# settle_cancel / settle_drain, which are native. A native promise is also
# drivable by a native tier as of the database boundary's flip, so the phrase
# could not have been kept even where a promise was really awaited.
#
